1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:06,538
How do you tell a shop about a payment without ever losing the news, or inventing it?

2
00:00:06,588 --> 00:00:11,923
Picture an app that saves a payment as paid, and then tells the shop.

3
00:00:11,973 --> 00:00:16,538
If it crashes in between, the shop never hears, and ships nothing.

4
00:00:16,588 --> 00:00:23,846
Swap the order, and a crash announces a payment that was never saved: the shop ships goods for free.

5
00:00:23,896 --> 00:00:26,923
An office solves this with an outbox tray.

6
00:00:26,973 --> 00:00:35,000
You close the file and drop the letter in the tray in one motion, and the postman collects from the tray.

7
00:00:35,050 --> 00:00:38,461
Here, the tray is a table called the outbox.

8
00:00:38,511 --> 00:00:45,769
When a payment ends, its event is written to the outbox in the same transaction as the final state.

9
00:00:45,819 --> 00:00:48,076
Both are saved, or neither is.

10
00:00:48,126 --> 00:00:52,307
A relay is the postman: it collects the oldest unsent rows.

11
00:00:52,357 --> 00:00:56,153
Two copies of the relay never pick the same row.

12
00:00:56,203 --> 00:01:02,692
It publishes each event to Kafka, keyed by its payment, so one payment's events stay in order.

13
00:01:02,742 --> 00:01:06,538
It marks a row sent only after Kafka has it.

14
00:01:06,588 --> 00:01:12,307
So a crash in between can send an event twice, and that is at-least-once delivery.

15
00:01:12,357 --> 00:01:17,307
Every event has its own id, so a repeat is easy to drop.

16
00:01:17,357 --> 00:01:21,538
The database stays the truth, and Kafka only carries the news.

17
00:01:21,588 --> 00:01:23,846
Now read it in the code.

18
00:01:23,896 --> 00:01:28,846
The outbox writer runs while the payment is saved, inside the same transaction.

19
00:01:28,896 --> 00:01:33,846
It saves one event row, with its own id, for each finished payment.

20
00:01:33,896 --> 00:01:39,615
The relay takes the oldest unpublished rows, and skips any row another relay has locked.

21
00:01:39,665 --> 00:01:43,461
And it marks them published only after they are sent.

22
00:01:43,511 --> 00:01:51,153
Like the outbox tray: decided and posted in one motion, so the postman may knock twice, but never zero times.

23
00:01:51,203 --> 00:01:54,230
Next: delivering the news to the merchant's server.
